  "explanation": "{\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"heading\": \"Warming Up With a Quick Win\",\n    \"body\": \"I started my morning with the easy puzzle and it was a total delight. The first thing that caught my eye was that big greater than 10 constraint covering cells 2,2 and 2,3. In a game like Pips, getting a total that high means you have to use your biggest dominoes. I looked at my pile and saw the 6,2 and the 3,3 pieces and knew they had to live around that area. \\n\\nAnother big clue was the empty spot at 3,2. When a cell is marked as empty, it really changes how you look at the surrounding dominoes. I had to be careful with how I placed the 3,2 domino itself. Once I figured out that the 1,1 domino fit perfectly in the corner where cell 1,1 needed to be less than 3, the rest of the board just kind of clicked into place. It felt like putting the last few pieces into a very small, satisfying jigsaw puzzle.\",\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"heading\": \"Finding the Rhythm in the Middle\",\n    \"body\": \"The medium puzzle today really made me think about my math for a second. I saw a few sum regions that acted as perfect anchors. For example, there was a sum of 2 at the top in cells 0,2 and 0,3. Since we are dealing with dominoes, that really limits your options. I realized I had to use the 1,2 domino or something similar nearby to make those totals work out. \\n\\nI spent a good chunk of time focusing on the right side of the board where cells 1,7 and 2,7 had to sum up to 11. That is a pretty big number! I had to look through my remaining pieces like the 5,5 and 6,5 to see what could actually reach that goal. Once I settled on the 6,5 domino for that spot, the equals constraint at 0,6 and 0,7 became much easier to handle. It is all about finding those high and low anchors first and then filling in the gaps.\",\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"heading\": \"Tackling the Saturday Giant\",\n    \"body\": \"Wow, the hard puzzle today was a real brain teaser! The standout feature was definitely that huge unequal region in the middle. Having five cells where no two numbers can be the same is always a challenge. I had to keep a mental checklist of which pips I had already used so I did not accidentally double up. I started at the very top and bottom because of the sum of 1 constraints at cells 0,0 and 9,0. Those are usually the easiest places to start because they narrow down your choices to just 0 and 1.\\n\\nThe breakthrough moment for me happened when I looked at the long equals region stretching across the bottom half. I had to balance the dominoes like 5,1 and 6,1 to make sure they matched up with the target values. There was a moment where I thought I had stuck myself in a corner with the 4,4 and 5,4 dominoes, but a quick swap fixed everything. It took a little more patience than usual, but seeing the whole grid filled out was such a great feeling.\",\n  },\n  \"tips\": \"Try starting with the smallest sum targets like 1 or 2 since they have very few possible combinations. Also, always look for the largest greater than constraints early on because they force you to use your high-value dominoes in specific spots, which clears up your inventory for the rest of the puzzle.\",\n  \"learned\": \"Today I really learned the value of the empty cell constraint. It is not just a blank space; it is a massive hint about which dominoes can and cannot go there. I also realized that the unequal regions are much easier to solve if you work from the outside in rather than starting right in the middle of the mess.\",\n  \"faqs\": [\n    {\n      \"question\": \"What does the empty constraint mean in NYT Pips?\",\n      \"answer\": \"An empty constraint usually means that specific cell has zero pips or is treated as a zero for any sum calculations in that region.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"How do you solve unequal regions in Pips puzzles?\",\n      \"answer\": \"The best way to handle these is to list out the numbers 0 through 6 and cross them off as you place dominoes in the region to ensure no numbers repeat.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"What is the best strategy for high sum targets?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Focus on your dominoes with the most pips, like the 6,6 or 5,6. High sums like 10 or 11 can usually only be made with a very small selection of pieces.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question\": \"Can dominoes be placed vertically or horizontally?\",\n      \"answer\": \"Yes, dominoes can be placed in either direction as long as they fit within the grid and satisfy the regional constraints like sums or equalities.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"
